#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269234 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184207 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1224e2dd-96bc-5c24-aacf-3c36a7c3a1e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Acute Angle Between a Vector and a Subspace"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a09b02d-8bbe-5d10-a4f9-370bef55e764" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{21.83}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266234} {:answer/id #uuid "60cc524b-fcb3-5eda-a2ef-972dc00b4dd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{20.02}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266999} {:answer/id #uuid "d0230632-643e-5176-9cfd-33172a36de8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{19.47}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268005} {:answer/id #uuid "e4a14d53-aae0-5887-9f9d-89614f80206d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{17.36}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268164} {:answer/id #uuid "e6ffd20f-b627-5d13-90b1-7aab917e72f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{18.88}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268192}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0230632-643e-5176-9cfd-33172a36de8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{19.47}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268005} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1122a16-f57b-5b34-8e12-fe56444b19bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268829}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a21d579a-3fab-5e90-bfa1-e3d7308d1aaf" :question/math-academy-id "q-139514" :question/problem "Find the acute angle between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRound the answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we get the following projection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ Equivalently, it is the acute angle between $x$ and any non-zero vector that is parallel to ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$ So, to make calculations easier, we can find the angle between the vectors\n\n$\\,$ $x=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\,{\\text{proj}}_{S}x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}x}{‖x‖‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot 1+(-5)\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+(-5)^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{54}\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{9\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{2}})≈{19.47}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369720 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184207 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1224e2dd-96bc-5c24-aacf-3c36a7c3a1e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Acute Angle Between a Vector and a Subspace"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1cac70a-4767-5747-aabc-96ad22e5a366" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{21.99}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369722} {:answer/id #uuid "e2ed8d05-f3ce-5e8b-8a39-f7d70814de29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{19.17}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369723} {:answer/id #uuid "0b1b8838-9e4f-5164-8943-3378b0c8d03b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{20.77}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369724} {:answer/id #uuid "e5d978de-0d75-5720-bbc9-579cb3cf2b16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{22.85}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369725} {:answer/id #uuid "1e8becfd-699d-50d6-8e7a-acbc243e9c0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{23.09}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369726}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e8becfd-699d-50d6-8e7a-acbc243e9c0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{23.09}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369726} :answer-field/id #uuid "a372a12b-f02d-574b-85c9-6ebecf5009c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369721}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "70bc7c7a-169e-56f1-a44c-50b8995c4794" :question/math-academy-id "q-139515" :question/problem "Find the acute angle between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRound the answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}9 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & \\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}x}{‖x‖‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2)\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot 3+0\\cdot (-1)}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{\\sqrt{13}\\sqrt{11}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{\\sqrt{13}})≈{23.09}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369727 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184176 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26b41192-9680-55c3-930c-52ff235e1800" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto the Solution Space of a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "151280e3-7bc9-572b-aedb-57349dd4556a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369729} {:answer/id #uuid "54b94dfb-0fb6-59af-b914-f9e3f34b68df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{9} \\\\ \\frac{2}{9}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369730} {:answer/id #uuid "00af225c-9d39-5d44-b013-9ecd351cdaaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{9} \\\\ \\frac{2}{9} \\\\ \\frac{2}{9}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369731} {:answer/id #uuid "f4fcf465-b7a5-595a-8687-891ac5987b16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369732} {:answer/id #uuid "77832619-b004-54fe-aac1-434e4ef9d15e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369733}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77832619-b004-54fe-aac1-434e4ef9d15e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369733} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b7e2ee6-1f7c-594d-b16e-4f2a770ccac3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369728}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d24d2ad1-e038-55ca-ab44-d3cbc23a789d" :question/math-academy-id "q-139518" :question/problem "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}],\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}-6{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find a basis for the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 6 & -6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}$ are free variables. Now, from the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B={[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is the basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nNow, we find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326788 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184207 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1224e2dd-96bc-5c24-aacf-3c36a7c3a1e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Acute Angle Between a Vector and a Subspace"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2935c965-3577-5dfe-ae8a-9b87acf0d993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{14.21}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324303} {:answer/id #uuid "477d3d8c-b227-51a2-94d4-fffe1c799e04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{54.05}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324551} {:answer/id #uuid "6a9c4cd1-8775-55a9-8f5f-e12814da13fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45.12}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324862} {:answer/id #uuid "8beb8a2b-fdbb-55aa-95bc-2738740a0bad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{34.05}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325161} {:answer/id #uuid "db5e5c22-3652-5b8c-b4b6-6526f0290ef4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{22.21}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db5e5c22-3652-5b8c-b4b6-6526f0290ef4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{22.21}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325883} :answer-field/id #uuid "83132e5b-ab8f-5767-b31f-36b261cf9c0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326456}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "21e1141a-cb0f-578b-8a6b-1baafee0f9ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-39877" :question/problem "Find the acute angle between the vector $x$ and the subspace spanned by the linearly independent vectors ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRound the answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we get the following projection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$   \n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {\\text{proj}}_{S}x}{‖x‖‖{\\text{proj}}_{S}x‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 2}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{14}\\sqrt{12}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{2\\sqrt{42}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{42}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{42}})≈{22.21}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394752 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184176 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26b41192-9680-55c3-930c-52ff235e1800" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto the Solution Space of a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0179cf73-eccd-5c14-981f-cceee10cca69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394754} {:answer/id #uuid "88717ff1-1cd0-54c7-a62e-843a486a23f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394755} {:answer/id #uuid "c88427fe-a2ca-560e-85f4-c030c8af9e45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394756} {:answer/id #uuid "85da7758-abe3-5b14-b3d6-2f47a05c1472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394757} {:answer/id #uuid "a982dee5-c867-531f-a6f7-f6593bdba5dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394758}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a982dee5-c867-531f-a6f7-f6593bdba5dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394758} :answer-field/id #uuid "e180b6ff-7854-527f-9bbf-6bafd67a09aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394753}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5cf24a3d-38a9-5fd8-a2a7-b75acfc0bac4" :question/math-academy-id "q-86846" :question/problem "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}],\\,{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}-9{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find a basis for the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 & -9 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}$ are free variables. Now, from the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}.$ Therefore, the general solution is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$ is the basis of the solution space of the system. \n\nNow, we find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}5 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 10\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-15 \\\\ -32\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{14}[\\begin{aligned}10 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-15 \\\\ -32\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{14}[\\begin{aligned}42 \\\\ -70\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
